cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Underpowered by Construction: Sharpe Differences of 0.1 Are Not
  Estimable from Realistic Samples
message: If you use this software or paper, please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Ethan
    family-names: Gao
repository-code: https://github.com/ohNwghtEG/macroeconomic-regime-detection-dashboard
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22737847
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22737847
    description: Concept DOI - resolves to the latest archived version
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22737848
    description: Version DOI for release v1.0-preprint
abstract: >-
  A macroeconomic regime-detection and asset-allocation pipeline built to
  institutional real-time standards - point-in-time data honouring each series'
  publication lag, forward-filtered hidden-Markov state probabilities, and
  expanding-window re-estimation - used to ask not whether regime models beat a
  static 60/40 but whether a backtest of realistic length could detect it if
  they did. Over 535 months no regime allocation differs significantly from a
  rebalanced 60/40, and a factorial ablation reintroducing three forms of
  look-ahead bias produces nothing surviving multiple-testing correction. A
  closed-form sample-size expression, derived from the Jobson-Korkie statistic
  with the Memmel correction and validated against simulation, shows why:
  detecting a 0.10 Sharpe difference requires between 128 and 610 years of
  monthly data depending on the correlation between strategy and benchmark.
keywords:
  - regime switching
  - hidden Markov models
  - backtesting
  - statistical power
  - Sharpe ratio inference
  - look-ahead bias
  - tactical asset allocation
  - reproducibility
